6\\).\n\nNext, we divide 682 by 216:\n\\[682 \\div 216 \\approx 3.161\\]\nThe integer part is 3, so the coefficient for \\(6^3\\) is 3. We then subtract \\(3 \\times 216 = 648\\) from 682:\n\\[682 - 648 = 34.\\]\n\nNow, we divide 34 by \\(6^2 = 36\\):\n\\[34 \\div 36 = 0.944\\]\nThe integer part is 0, so the coefficient for \\(6^2\\) is 0. We then subtract \\(0 \\times 36 = 0\\) from 34:\n\\[34 - 0 = 34.\\]\n\nNext, we divide 34 by \\(6^1 = 6\\):\n\\[34 \\div 6 = 5.667\\]\nThe integer part is 5, so the coefficient for \\(6^1\\) is 5. We then subtract \\(5 \\times 6 = 30\\) from 34:\n\\[34 - 30 = 4.\\]\n\nFinally, the coefficient for \\(6^0\\) is 4. So, the base-6 representation of \\(682_{10}\\) is \\(3054_6\\).\n\nStep 2: Identify the first and last digits of the base-6 representation.\nThe first digit is 3 and the last digit is 4.\n\nStep 3: Calculate the product of the first and last digits.\n\\[3 \\times 4 = 12.\\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the first and last digits of the base-6 representation of \\(682_{10}\\) is \\(\\boxed{12}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{12}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:16"}
